24451:
At the highest point the vertical velocity vanishes, leaving v_x = v \\cos 60^\\circ = 20 \\times 0.5 = 10 \\text{ m/s}
Motion is perpendicular to acceleration g there, so g acts fully as normal acceleration a_n = 10 \\text{ m/s}^2
Radius of curvature R = v^2 / a_n = 10^2 / 10 = 10 \\text{ m} → (A)

24454:
Torque magnitude is \\tau = pE \\sin\\theta with \\theta = 30^\\circ
Substituting gives \\tau = (4 \\times 10^{-9})(5 \\times 10^4)(0.5) = 10 \\times 10^{-5}
Hence \\tau = 1.0 \\times 10^{-4} \\text{ N m} → (A)

24455:
Field at the centre is B = \\mu_0 N I / (2R)
With R = 0.10 \\text{ m} this gives B = (4\\pi \\times 10^{-7})(50)(2) / (0.20) = 2\\pi \\times 10^{-4}
Numerically 2 \\times 3.14 \\times 10^{-4} = 6.28 \\times 10^{-4} \\text{ T} → (A)

24459:
Mass to supply is \\Delta m = 4(4.002) - 15.348 = 0.66 \\text{ amu}
Energy E = \\Delta m c^2 = 0.66 \\times 1.66 \\times 10^{-27} \\times (3 \\times 10^8)^2 = 9.86 \\times 10^{-11} \\text{ J}
Frequency \\nu = E / h = 9.86 \\times 10^{-11} / (6.6 \\times 10^{-34}) = 1.49 \\times 10^{23} \\text{ Hz} = 1.49 \\times 10^{20} \\text{ kHz}
This equals 14.9 \\times 10^{19} \\text{ kHz} → (D)

24461:
Equilateral prism has A = 60^\\circ, and grazing emergence means r_2 equals the critical angle with \\sin r_2 = 1 / \\mu = 1 / \\sqrt{2}
So r_2 = 45^\\circ and r_1 = A - r_2 = 60 - 45 = 15
The refraction angle at entry is 15^\\circ → (A)

24469:
Escape speed scales as v_e = \\sqrt{2GM / R} = R\\sqrt{8\\pi G\\rho / 3}, so v_e \\propto R\\sqrt{\\rho}
Ratio is v_B / v_A = 0.1 \\times \\sqrt{0.1} = 1 / \\sqrt{1000}
Hence v_B = 10000 / \\sqrt{1000} = 100\\sqrt{10} \\text{ m/s} → (C)

24474:
Medium speed is v = \\omega / k = 4.5 \\times 10^{14} / (3 \\times 10^6) = 1.5 \\times 10^8 \\text{ m/s}
Refractive index n = c / v = 3 \\times 10^8 / (1.5 \\times 10^8) = 2
Nonmagnetic medium has K = n^2 = 4 → (4)

24478:
\text{NO} has 7 + 8 = 15 electrons with the configuration ending in a single electron in \pi^*_{2p}
Bonding electrons N_b = 10 and antibonding electrons N_a = 5 give BO = (10 - 5) / 2 = 2.5
The lone \pi^* electron makes it paramagnetic, while \text{CO} and \text{N}_{2} have BO 3 fully paired and \text{O}_{2} has BO 2 → (A)

24483:
Fehling solution is reduced only by aliphatic aldehydes, depositing red \text{Cu}_{2}\text{O}
Acetaldehyde has the oxidizable -CHO group on an alkyl fragment, while benzaldehyde is resonance stabilized and ketones lack the aldehydic H
Hence only acetaldehyde gives a positive test → (A)

24492:
Excitation n = 1 to n = 2 needs \Delta E = R_{H} (1 - 1 / 4) = 0.75 R_{H} per atom
Per atom this is 0.75 \times 2.18 \times 10^{-18} = 1.635 \times 10^{-18} \text{ J}
Per mole multiply by N_{A} to get 1.635 \times 10^{-18} \times 6.023 \times 10^{23} = 9.85 \times 10^{5} \text{ J/mol}
Nearest option is 9.835 \times 10^{5} → (A)

24493:
\text{Br}_{2} with \text{KOH} is the Hoffmann bromamide step, which only primary amides undergo, so A is Ar-\text{CONH}_{2}
Diazotization plus \text{CuCN} swaps -\text{NH}_{2} for -CN, and hydrolysis turns -CN into -\text{COOH}
Since E is p-toluic acid and F is terephthalic acid, the methyl and amide groups are para to each other
Hence A is 4-methylbenzamide → (A)

24497:
Silver iodide has M = 108 + 127 = 235 with 127 parts iodine
Iodine mass in the precipitate is (127 / 235) \times 0.12 = 0.0649 \text{ g}
Share in the 0.15 \text{ g} sample is (0.0649 / 0.15) \times 100 = 43.2, nearest integer 43 → (43)

24498:
Standard EMF is E^\circ_{\text{cell}} = -0.44 - (-0.90) = +0.46 \text{ V} with cathode bismuth and anode tin
The six electron change gives E_{\text{cell}} = 0.46 - (0.059 / 6) \log(10^{6}) = 0.46 - 0.059 = 0.401 \text{ V}
This is 4.01 \times 10^{-1} \text{ V}, nearest integer 4 → (4)
